104
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:28,800
So you're here about the vandalism?

105
00:06:29,080 --> 00:06:30,240
It was in the papers.

106
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:31,920
Oh, good, you know of it.

107
00:06:32,300 --> 00:06:35,840
I'm sorry to say, the local police have been of no use in this affair.

108
00:06:36,340 --> 00:06:40,100
Which is why I thought I might enlist the help of the greatest detective in all London,

109
00:06:40,340 --> 00:06:41,500
Mr. Sherlock Holmes.

110
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:47,780
Of course, I'm more than happy to pay for whatever services you may provide.

111
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:50,340
I've set aside three hundred pounds.

112
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:52,500
Three hundred pounds?

113
00:06:52,820 --> 00:06:55,680
That would cover the cost of repairs from the time we blew up the flat.

114
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,500
And Miss Hudson might find it in her heart to get off our backs.

115
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:10,060
The whole affair did sound quite intriguing.

116
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:11,880
Oh, we should be off.

117
00:07:12,220 --> 00:07:14,680
It's almost time for our appointment with Baron Cupid.

118
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:16,500
Ah, so it is.

119
00:07:16,700 --> 00:07:18,340
An appointment with the Baron, you say?

120
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:19,980
Indeed.

121
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:23,240
I thought, why not see the painting with my own eyes?

122
00:07:23,460 --> 00:07:26,900
If it's not an imposition, Lewis and I would love to join you.

123
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:36,360
Mr. Holmes, Dr. Watson, it's a pleasure.

124
00:07:36,860 --> 00:07:38,480
Murray has told me all about you.

125
00:07:39,340 --> 00:07:41,100
I, of course, am Baron Cupid.

126
00:07:41,100 --> 00:07:44,800
But who are these gentlemen?

127
00:07:45,020 --> 00:07:50,420
These are my friends, the brothers Moriarty, William in the top hat and Lewis in the bowler.

128
00:07:51,240 --> 00:07:52,900
I bumped into them in town.

129
00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:55,100
They would also like to see your painting.

130
00:07:55,800 --> 00:07:57,080
I trust that won't be a problem.

131
00:07:58,420 --> 00:08:00,040
Pleasure to meet you, Lord Cupid.

132
00:08:01,140 --> 00:08:03,320
William James Moriarty at your service.

133
00:08:04,140 --> 00:08:06,660
I hope you'll forgive our dropping by uninvited.

134
00:08:07,480 --> 00:08:08,120
Oh, please.

135
00:08:08,660 --> 00:08:10,840
It's no trouble at all, Lord Moriarty.

136
00:08:15,900 --> 00:08:17,140
This is appalling.

137
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:21,960
I hope you'll pardon me for asking, but how much do your paintings usually sell for?

138
00:08:22,620 --> 00:08:23,880
It depends on the piece.

139
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:27,200
But they often command in the range of 500 pounds.

140
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:28,100
500?

141
00:08:28,660 --> 00:08:32,980
Then perhaps the culprit is simply displeased with your work being so profitable.

142
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:37,620
If they know what they're worth, then they should know I donate the proceeds in full to local charities.

143
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,060
I receive no profit from my work.

144
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:45,800
Sir, I'm curious whether you might be willing to show us around your atelier.

145
00:08:47,140 --> 00:08:47,940
What for?

146
00:08:49,280 --> 00:08:51,320
Is this an inopportune time?

147
00:08:51,940 --> 00:08:53,380
No, it's not a bother.

148
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:57,320
It's just a bit of a track away, but if you gentlemen will follow me...

149
00:09:03,320 --> 00:09:18,640
My, you certainly keep it tidy in here.

150
00:09:18,640 --> 00:09:22,640
Yes, I find it difficult to concentrate when things are cluttered.

151
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:29,120
This is unusual.

152
00:09:29,860 --> 00:09:30,460
What is?

153
00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:34,040
This pigment includes lead as one of its components.

154
00:09:34,720 --> 00:09:36,980
You hardly ever see this sort of thing nowadays.

155
00:09:36,980 --> 00:09:43,840
I know it's rare, but to me, no other paint is suitable for white lilies.

156
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:48,640
Besides, nothing beats painting with what you're accustomed to.

157
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:50,440
I see.

158
00:09:51,020 --> 00:09:53,720
So this is the secret to the beauty of your work.

159
00:09:54,200 --> 00:09:54,700
Yes.

160
00:09:54,700 --> 00:10:03,160
My sketchbook.

161
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:05,140
That's odd.

162
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:07,540
I was quite sure I put that away.

163
00:10:07,540 --> 00:10:26,240
The failings by which I'm quite embarrassed, forgive me.

164
00:10:26,980 --> 00:10:32,240
And to be perfectly frank with you, I've had a devil of a time concentrating on anything ever since the incident occurred.

165
00:10:32,820 --> 00:10:34,220
Yes, I'd imagine, sir.

166
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:35,960
I'm terribly sorry.

167
00:10:36,280 --> 00:10:37,980
I shouldn't have insisted we come.

168
00:10:38,980 --> 00:10:41,140
On the contrary, I'm grateful to you for asking.

169
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:43,760
But I do think it's time we return to the house.

170
00:10:44,200 --> 00:10:45,120
Yes, very well.

171
00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:56,980
So, did you notice anything, Sherlock?

172
00:10:57,700 --> 00:10:58,440
Let's see.

173
00:10:59,300 --> 00:11:04,020
Based on my initial observations, I believe that our culprit is left-handed and quite short.

174
00:11:04,220 --> 00:11:05,380
How do you figure that?

175
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:09,600
Our defaced painting suffered little damage to the upper half.

176
00:11:09,880 --> 00:11:11,900
The lower half bore the brunt of the assault.

177
00:11:12,180 --> 00:11:17,220
But based on its position in the gallery, it's likely our vandal had to use a stepladder to reach it.

178
00:11:17,220 --> 00:11:28,100
I also noticed several fine lines tracked through the paint underneath the highest brushstrokes, indicating that as they stretched out their arm, their sleeve, or possibly a lapel, rubbed against the canvas.

179
00:11:28,700 --> 00:11:31,160
But what I can't tell is what drove them to do it.

180
00:11:33,080 --> 00:11:36,880
Liam, why did you prompt Lord Cubitt to shake your hand?

181
00:11:36,880 --> 00:11:42,520
I simply wish to follow proper etiquette, as it was my first time to meet the gentleman.

182
00:11:43,240 --> 00:11:43,400
Ha.

183
00:11:43,940 --> 00:11:44,380
Funny.

184
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:46,360
That's not at all what it looked like to me.

185
00:11:47,700 --> 00:11:50,980
Cubitt's hands showed no signs of the man being an expert artist.

186
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:54,120
No calluses, no pigments, staining his cuticles.

187
00:11:54,460 --> 00:11:57,340
In other words, that man simply does not paint.

188
00:11:58,220 --> 00:11:58,620
What?

189
00:11:58,620 --> 00:12:06,660
Assuming that's what the handshake was meant to discover, I think you know much more than you've let on.

190
00:12:10,360 --> 00:12:11,280
My goodness.

191
00:12:11,840 --> 00:12:13,960
You think far too highly of my skills.

192
00:12:14,220 --> 00:12:16,600
I'm merely a long-time fan of Lord Cubitt's work.

193
00:12:17,220 --> 00:12:21,340
However, I do know that you are hiding something, Mr. Holmes.

194
00:12:21,700 --> 00:12:21,820
Hmm?

195
00:12:22,260 --> 00:12:22,880
Are you not?

196
00:12:23,560 --> 00:12:24,880
Tucked away in your pocket.

197
00:12:26,940 --> 00:12:28,440
You caught that, did you?

198
00:12:28,620 --> 00:12:29,640
I'm impressed.

199
00:12:30,160 --> 00:12:32,540
I discovered this inside his sketchbook.

200
00:12:34,020 --> 00:12:42,340
A series of numbers stained with the same paint the culprit used, which means it's very likely a message.

201
00:12:46,380 --> 00:12:48,940
John and I are going to work on the meaning of this note.

202
00:12:49,780 --> 00:12:50,700
What about you two?

203
00:12:51,520 --> 00:12:55,580
I think Louis and I will enjoy a nice stroll as we make our way back to the hotel.

204
00:12:56,260 --> 00:12:56,740
Right.

205
00:12:56,740 --> 00:12:59,420
You did come here to see the sights, after all.

206
00:12:59,960 --> 00:13:02,540
Then I suppose this is goodbye once again.

207
00:13:03,320 --> 00:13:03,700
Indeed.

208
00:13:04,500 --> 00:13:05,440
Till next time.

209
00:13:13,640 --> 00:13:14,120
Louis.

210
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:18,480
I apologize for dragging you along whilst I indulged myself.

211
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:19,500
Oh, please.

212
00:13:19,500 --> 00:13:19,520
Oh, please.

213
00:13:19,520 --> 00:13:21,080
Don't give it another thought.

214
00:13:21,980 --> 00:13:24,100
Though I did notice something rather odd.

215
00:13:24,780 --> 00:13:28,000
When we met Lord Cubitt, he had no reaction at all.

216
00:13:28,540 --> 00:13:29,500
Which would indicate...

217
00:13:30,500 --> 00:13:30,980
Correct.

218
00:13:31,540 --> 00:13:33,820
That is not the man I met in the slums.

219
00:13:37,620 --> 00:13:38,020
Louis.

220
00:13:38,700 --> 00:13:38,960
Hmm?

221
00:13:38,960 --> 00:13:44,780
If you will, I have a favor to ask you.

222
00:13:44,780 --> 00:13:44,800
If you will, I have a favor to ask you.

223
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:50,660
Little man.

224
00:13:50,660 --> 00:13:53,320
No time.

225
00:13:53,660 --> 00:13:54,020
No time.

226
00:13:54,020 --> 00:13:55,360
You are not about your back.

227
00:13:55,920 --> 00:13:56,300
No time.

228
00:13:56,340 --> 00:13:56,720
No time.

229
00:13:57,380 --> 00:13:57,680
Okay.

230
00:13:58,020 --> 00:13:58,100
No time.

231
00:14:02,520 --> 00:14:02,560
No time.

232
00:14:02,560 --> 00:14:03,260
All minds.

233
00:14:03,340 --> 00:14:03,380
Let's go.

234
00:14:03,380 --> 00:14:05,160
First time.

235
00:14:05,300 --> 00:14:05,580
Your turn.

236
00:14:06,860 --> 00:14:06,940
Evil.

237
00:14:06,940 --> 00:14:07,100
Yo.

238
00:14:07,100 --> 00:14:08,060
If you will, you will hear me.

239
00:14:09,140 --> 00:14:09,600
That am救.

240
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:10,020
I will hear you.

241
00:14:10,860 --> 00:14:11,300
Yes.

242
00:14:11,300 --> 00:14:11,600
Thank you.

243
00:14:11,600 --> 00:14:17,240
My lord, a letter has arrived for you.

244
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:18,780
Ah, much obliged.

245
00:14:34,360 --> 00:14:35,300
It's Cubit.

246
00:14:35,820 --> 00:14:36,940
I received your letter.

247
00:14:37,880 --> 00:14:39,800
I don't need to know who you are,

248
00:14:39,800 --> 00:14:42,660
but please tell me how you know...

249
00:14:42,660 --> 00:14:44,580
What on earth are you doing here?

250
00:14:44,980 --> 00:14:46,080
I could ask the same.

251
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:48,400
What is a child doing in my atelier?

252
00:14:54,560 --> 00:14:56,180
I want to talk to you.

253
00:14:56,960 --> 00:14:58,740
I'll meet you at his atelier tonight.

254
00:14:59,440 --> 00:15:00,920
From Stanley's friend.

255
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:03,780
I received the exact same letter.

256
00:15:04,680 --> 00:15:05,960
Do you know Alan?

257
00:15:06,560 --> 00:15:07,740
Uh, Mr. Stanley?

258
00:15:07,740 --> 00:15:11,680
I used to be a fan of your paintings, you know that?

259
00:15:12,920 --> 00:15:14,480
When there were deliveries for you,

260
00:15:15,100 --> 00:15:16,620
I took them as often as I could.

261
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:20,460
So one day when I saw someone walking from your house into the woods,

262
00:15:20,980 --> 00:15:22,680
I was sure it must have been you.

263
00:15:23,180 --> 00:15:24,320
So I followed after them.

264
00:15:25,760 --> 00:15:26,740
But then...

265
00:15:26,740 --> 00:15:39,220
That was when I became friends with Mr. Stanley.

266
00:15:39,760 --> 00:15:41,240
And I know that you tricked him.

267
00:15:41,460 --> 00:15:43,440
Don't think you can hide what you did any longer.

268
00:15:43,940 --> 00:15:45,400
Once you got your money in vain,

269
00:15:45,460 --> 00:15:47,320
you didn't need Mr. Stanley anymore.

270
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:48,420
Stop this.

271
00:15:48,560 --> 00:15:49,600
You don't know what you're saying.

272
00:15:49,780 --> 00:15:50,100
He was...

273
00:15:54,100 --> 00:15:56,360
I really didn't want to ruin that painting.

274
00:15:56,740 --> 00:15:59,220
But I thought at least you wouldn't be able to use that one

275
00:15:59,220 --> 00:16:00,560
to make your filthy fortune.

276
00:16:01,440 --> 00:16:03,800
And now I'll see that you wait once and for all!

277
00:16:04,460 --> 00:16:04,640
What?

278
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:08,640
Good sir.

279
00:16:08,820 --> 00:16:11,200
You needn't stain your hands with blood at your age.

280
00:16:12,400 --> 00:16:13,500
Why, Lord Moriarty?

281
00:16:16,420 --> 00:16:18,300
You already have stains enough.

282
00:16:19,580 --> 00:16:21,520
When I saw you in the gallery yesterday,

283
00:16:22,100 --> 00:16:24,480
I admit I was baffled as to why your left sleeve

284
00:16:24,480 --> 00:16:25,940
was so much shorter than the right.

285
00:16:26,620 --> 00:16:28,180
But once I saw the painting,

286
00:16:28,400 --> 00:16:29,940
it all made perfect sense.

287
00:16:30,220 --> 00:16:30,720
Get off!

288
00:16:31,120 --> 00:16:32,380
What do you want with me?

289
00:16:32,380 --> 00:16:35,960
I was the one who sent those letters to you.

290
00:16:36,860 --> 00:16:37,700
You were?

291
00:16:37,880 --> 00:16:39,200
Then that means...

292
00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:40,740
You're Mr. Stanley's friend.

293
00:16:41,880 --> 00:16:42,440
Unfortunately,

294
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:44,900
Mr. Alan Stanley has passed away.

295
00:16:45,400 --> 00:16:46,340
But you should know,

296
00:16:46,920 --> 00:16:48,760
his death was not Lord Qubit's fault.

297
00:16:49,120 --> 00:16:49,360
What?

298
00:16:50,860 --> 00:16:52,500
Liam is correct.

299
00:16:53,320 --> 00:16:54,340
Mr. Holmes.

300
00:16:54,880 --> 00:16:55,660
My friend,

301
00:16:55,900 --> 00:16:57,540
you could have been less cryptic.

302
00:16:59,180 --> 00:17:01,700
The cause of this Stanley chap's death

303
00:17:01,700 --> 00:17:02,560
was illness.

304
00:17:03,680 --> 00:17:05,320
Wasn't it, Lord Qubit?

305
00:17:06,080 --> 00:17:07,300
Yes, indeed it was.

306
00:17:07,660 --> 00:17:10,380
I'm afraid he was eventually done in by Saturnism.

307
00:17:11,080 --> 00:17:11,960
By what?

308
00:17:12,920 --> 00:17:13,820
Lead poisoning.

309
00:17:14,340 --> 00:17:16,480
From the pigments he used to mix his paint.

310
00:17:17,140 --> 00:17:19,820
As the illness enters its final stages,

311
00:17:20,180 --> 00:17:22,900
one sees symptoms that include intellectual decline,

312
00:17:23,180 --> 00:17:23,820
irritability,

313
00:17:23,820 --> 00:17:25,120
and hand tremors.

314
00:17:25,120 --> 00:17:29,180
Which naturally explains why the pictures in his sketchbook

315
00:17:29,180 --> 00:17:31,300
grew increasingly worse over time.

316
00:17:32,820 --> 00:17:34,680
The only way to treat him

317
00:17:34,680 --> 00:17:36,940
was to stop the exposure.

318
00:17:38,100 --> 00:17:39,380
But he refused.

319
00:17:40,180 --> 00:17:42,660
He chose to keep painting until the very end,

320
00:17:42,920 --> 00:17:44,020
despite his symptoms.

321
00:17:44,020 --> 00:17:48,640
If only I had tried harder,

322
00:17:49,080 --> 00:17:50,120
forced him to rest,

323
00:17:50,700 --> 00:17:52,720
perhaps my dear friend

324
00:17:52,720 --> 00:17:54,660
would not have had to suffer the way he did.

325
00:17:55,060 --> 00:17:56,320
You were truly friends?

326
00:17:57,200 --> 00:17:57,820
We were.

327
00:17:58,180 --> 00:17:59,680
He was the son of the stable hand

328
00:17:59,680 --> 00:18:01,000
at the villa we had in London

329
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:01,980
when I was a child.

330
00:18:03,320 --> 00:18:05,080
We were roughly the same age,

331
00:18:05,540 --> 00:18:06,800
but my parents told me

332
00:18:06,800 --> 00:18:08,840
I was not to interact with commoners.

333
00:18:08,840 --> 00:18:10,420
So, for many years,

334
00:18:10,540 --> 00:18:11,440
we barely spoke.

335
00:18:12,660 --> 00:18:13,460
But one day,

336
00:18:13,880 --> 00:18:15,440
I just happened to see

337
00:18:15,440 --> 00:18:16,440
one of his paintings.

338
00:18:17,760 --> 00:18:19,500
I fell in love with his work.

339
00:18:20,100 --> 00:18:22,000
And that brought us together.

340
00:18:23,900 --> 00:18:25,000
You know, George,

341
00:18:25,180 --> 00:18:26,120
it's a bit sad,

342
00:18:26,300 --> 00:18:28,240
but sometimes I wish there was a place

343
00:18:28,240 --> 00:18:30,740
where even the poor could seek an education,

344
00:18:31,680 --> 00:18:33,660
regardless of money or status.

345
00:18:34,440 --> 00:18:37,120
But I know that dream is still an age away.

346
00:18:37,480 --> 00:18:38,300
No, it isn't.

347
00:18:38,300 --> 00:18:40,340
Listen, Alan,

348
00:18:40,600 --> 00:18:42,420
you and your paintings have value.

349
00:18:42,820 --> 00:18:44,760
You can make that happen in your lifetime.

350
00:18:45,020 --> 00:18:45,520
I know it.

351
00:18:47,340 --> 00:18:47,900
However,

352
00:18:48,400 --> 00:18:50,220
the years slipped by

353
00:18:50,220 --> 00:18:52,180
and he never sold a single painting.

354
00:18:54,220 --> 00:18:55,520
Why don't we say

355
00:18:55,520 --> 00:18:57,620
you're the one who painted my pieces?

356
00:18:59,200 --> 00:19:00,260
Why on earth?

357
00:19:01,060 --> 00:19:02,320
My work won't sell

358
00:19:02,320 --> 00:19:03,840
because a commoner's name

359
00:19:03,840 --> 00:19:05,060
is attached to it all.

360
00:19:05,060 --> 00:19:08,140
I don't need fame.

361
00:19:08,860 --> 00:19:11,100
I only need you to promise me one thing.

362
00:19:11,440 --> 00:19:13,180
Use their earnings to help the poor.

363
00:19:14,060 --> 00:19:15,620
And I'll be your ghost painter

364
00:19:15,620 --> 00:19:16,940
for as long as you wish.

365
00:19:17,940 --> 00:19:18,920
I swear it.

366
00:19:20,520 --> 00:19:21,520
What do you say?

367
00:19:23,140 --> 00:19:24,060
Alan, I...

368
00:19:24,060 --> 00:19:36,560
And so we relocated our base of operations

369
00:19:36,560 --> 00:19:37,960
to my hometown of Bath

370
00:19:37,960 --> 00:19:40,400
where we sold his paintings under my name.

371
00:19:40,400 --> 00:19:44,340
But it still doesn't add up.

372
00:19:44,820 --> 00:19:46,360
You haven't explained those numbers.

373
00:19:46,940 --> 00:19:47,520
So go on.

374
00:19:47,780 --> 00:19:48,760
Make it make sense.

375
00:19:49,260 --> 00:19:49,780
Numbers?

376
00:19:50,280 --> 00:19:51,940
He's referring to this.

377
00:19:51,940 --> 00:19:54,020
It compares the final sale

378
00:19:54,020 --> 00:19:55,440
of several recent paintings

379
00:19:55,440 --> 00:19:56,600
with the amount set aside

380
00:19:56,600 --> 00:19:58,040
for donations to charity.

381
00:19:59,000 --> 00:19:59,980
Despite the claim

382
00:19:59,980 --> 00:20:01,460
that you donate all the proceeds

383
00:20:01,460 --> 00:20:02,320
from your sales,

384
00:20:02,640 --> 00:20:03,240
the truth is

385
00:20:03,240 --> 00:20:04,280
you've only ever contributed

386
00:20:04,280 --> 00:20:05,240
a few percent.

387
00:20:06,820 --> 00:20:08,560
And the reason you found out

388
00:20:08,560 --> 00:20:09,460
about the difference

389
00:20:09,460 --> 00:20:10,240
is likely

390
00:20:10,240 --> 00:20:11,540
that you've been peeking

391
00:20:11,540 --> 00:20:12,140
at the letters

392
00:20:12,140 --> 00:20:13,700
being sent from Cubitt Manor.

393
00:20:14,980 --> 00:20:16,300
I know that what I did

394
00:20:16,300 --> 00:20:17,240
was wrong, of course.

395
00:20:17,860 --> 00:20:19,540
But I thought maybe I could find out

396
00:20:19,540 --> 00:20:20,560
what happened to Mr. Stanley.

397
00:20:20,560 --> 00:20:23,740
And then I discovered

398
00:20:23,740 --> 00:20:24,840
a donation check.

399
00:20:26,080 --> 00:20:27,540
So I asked your art dealer

400
00:20:27,540 --> 00:20:28,900
and the aristocrats at the spa

401
00:20:28,900 --> 00:20:30,260
about the price of your paintings.

402
00:20:30,900 --> 00:20:32,020
And I found out

403
00:20:32,020 --> 00:20:33,680
the amounts were completely different.

404
00:20:34,160 --> 00:20:35,160
So then...

405
00:20:35,160 --> 00:20:35,500
Indeed.

406
00:20:35,960 --> 00:20:36,960
You and Mr. Stanley

407
00:20:36,960 --> 00:20:38,700
were being fed false sale prices

408
00:20:38,700 --> 00:20:39,380
from the beginning.

409
00:20:40,500 --> 00:20:42,020
The man behind it all

410
00:20:42,020 --> 00:20:42,880
is your art dealer.

411
00:20:43,440 --> 00:20:45,200
He's made a small fortune off of you

412
00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:46,780
simply from pocketing the difference.

413
00:20:47,680 --> 00:20:49,240
And it seems he's had a hand

414
00:20:49,240 --> 00:20:50,540
in other dark dealings.

415
00:20:50,560 --> 00:20:51,060
as well.

416
00:20:53,520 --> 00:20:54,380
Hold it, Vlad!

417
00:20:56,880 --> 00:20:57,720
Get back!

418
00:20:58,200 --> 00:20:58,680
How?

419
00:20:58,980 --> 00:21:00,500
How does this not upset you?

420
00:21:02,320 --> 00:21:03,900
If you truly knew Stanley,

421
00:21:04,020 --> 00:21:05,000
then you should understand

422
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
this isn't the sort of thing

423
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:07,860
he would have wanted.

424
00:21:08,820 --> 00:21:09,840
The path of vengeance

425
00:21:09,840 --> 00:21:10,780
is a misery

426
00:21:10,780 --> 00:21:11,860
you needn't suffer,

427
00:21:12,080 --> 00:21:12,520
my friend.

428
00:21:12,520 --> 00:21:13,920
This is the finish thing.

429
00:21:30,920 --> 00:21:31,800
Is that the question?

430
00:21:31,800 --> 00:21:36,260
Blimey, you policemen really are good for absolutely nothing, aren't you?

431
00:21:37,020 --> 00:21:39,500
So what if my reported sale prices were incorrect?

432
00:21:39,940 --> 00:21:41,520
We all make mistakes, don't we?

433
00:21:42,040 --> 00:21:43,960
I'll sue you for wrongful arrest.

434
00:21:44,200 --> 00:21:45,500
Jean-Paul Marais.

435
00:21:46,340 --> 00:21:50,080
Sorry to interrupt, but we're here to take you in on suspicion of treason.

436
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:51,000
For what?

437
00:21:52,140 --> 00:21:55,640
This man has been using his position as an art dealer as a cover.

438
00:21:56,060 --> 00:21:59,480
He is an arms dealer for nations hostile to the crown.

439
00:22:01,800 --> 00:22:06,380
Naturally, you have the right to silence.

440
00:22:07,380 --> 00:22:08,920
I suggest that you use it.

441
00:22:15,040 --> 00:22:23,120
You know, a friendship between a philanthropic nobleman and a destitute artist is rather heartwarming.

442
00:22:23,780 --> 00:22:29,300
Though tell me, Liam, why is it that you seem to actually know this Alan Stanley chap yourself?

443
00:22:31,800 --> 00:22:39,980
They're beautiful.

444
00:22:41,120 --> 00:22:42,880
Flowers blooming in the gutter.

445
00:22:43,160 --> 00:22:43,760
I like it.

446
00:22:44,380 --> 00:22:44,940
Thank you.

447
00:22:44,940 --> 00:22:50,060
My greatest hope for these works is that their purity can inspire change in people.

448
00:22:51,020 --> 00:22:56,040
If these lilies can drive even one man to kindness, then I'm quite positive.

449
00:22:56,800 --> 00:22:58,900
That will change the entire world.

450
00:23:02,020 --> 00:23:03,340
Does that sound odd?

451
00:23:03,340 --> 00:23:06,020
No.

452
00:23:07,020 --> 00:23:08,480
I happen to agree.

453
00:23:09,940 --> 00:23:11,380
I did know of him.

454
00:23:11,880 --> 00:23:14,720
Unfortunately, I can't claim to have truly known the gentleman.

455
00:23:15,500 --> 00:23:15,940
Oh.

456
00:23:18,580 --> 00:23:21,860
Well, I suppose I'll leave it at that, then.

457
00:23:21,860 --> 00:23:44,600
Let's go, 2, 3, 1, 2, 2...

